  <!-- POST 5: My Story (English) -->
  <item>
    <title>First Night in Cox's Bazar</title>
    <link>https://animeshbiswas.com/2024/09/12/first-night-coxs-bazar/</link>
    <pubDate>Thu, 12 Sep 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
    <dc:creator><![CDATA[animesh]]></dc:creator>
    <guid isPermaLink="false">https://animeshbiswas.com/?p=201</guid>
    <description></description>
    <content:encoded><![CDATA[
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em>A small notebook entry from the early days of the Rohingya response.</em></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The first night in Cox's Bazar, I could not sleep. Not from the heat, not from the briefings, not from anything I could name in a report. It was the smell of damp tarpaulin in the air, and the sound of generators running half a kilometre away, and the way the moon looked the same as it did in my own neighbourhood in Dhaka, but somehow not.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>I had brought a small notebook. I wrote one line that night:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote">
  <p><em>People are not numbers, but tonight everyone is counting.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It is many years later now, and I have learned that the difference between a death surveillance system and a memorial is mostly a matter of follow-through.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
]]></content:encoded>
    <excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[A small notebook entry from the early days of the Rohingya response.]]></excerpt:encoded>
    <wp:post_id>201</wp:post_id>
